  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 206 PM MDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTemperatures have soared early today with weak inversions and\nfurther warming aloft. We'll be pushing 100F or a bit more across\nmost of the plains and I-25 Corridor through late this afternoon,\nbefore finally some slight relief arrives over the next few days.\nThe Heat Advisory for the I-25 Urban Corridor through Weld county\nwill be allowed to expire at 9 pm this evening. \n\nScattered storms were developing in the northern mountains as \nexpected this afternoon. While the majority of the storms stay in \nthe high country, isolated high based convection with gusty winds\nand light rain may also spill onto the plains into this evening as\nthe hot temperatures help break the cap.  \n\nFor Tuesday, the upper level high directly overhead will elongate\nand shift to the southeast. This will allow for a slight bump in \nmonsoonal moisture as flow aloft turns light southerly. The \nmoisture bump would be much greater, however, if it wasn't for the\ncurrent dry punch into southern Colorado seen in the waver vapor \nimagery. A \"cool\" front will back into Colorado late tonight and \nearly Tuesday morning. This will support a slight increase of low \nlevel moisture over the plains as well. Precipitable water (PW) \nvalues still increase to 150-175% of normal over the higher \nterrain, so locally heavy rainfall will be possible. The main \nthreat of any flooding would be over the most severe portions of \nthe 2020 burn scars and the 2024 Alexander burn scar, given an \ninch of rain would be possible in 30 minutes from the strongest \nstorms. The best chance of storms on the plains would be late in \nthe day and especially in the evening as east/southeast low level \nflow continues to support low level moisture advection into slow \nmoving storms. Given the very warm temperatures, these will likely\nbe outflow dominated so any heavier rain may be relatively short-\nlived, but there is an opportunity for more balanced storms and\nlocally heavy rain especially if they persist later into the \nevening.\n\nThe greatest risk for locally heavy rainfall and any threat of \nflash flooding will enter the picture for Wednesday and Thursday. \nPW values are still forecast to reach the daily maxes for this \ntime of year, with ensemble averages generally pointing to 1.3-1.4\ninches along the I-25 Corridor, and 150-200% above normal across  \nthe entire forecast area. That's the common ingredient for heavy \nrainfall potential for both days, but each day will also have \ntheir own characteristics that may be more/less favorable for \nheavy rain. \n\nOn Wednesday, we'll have very slow storm motions, but rather \nshallow warm cloud depth less than 3,000 feet. On Thursday, warm\ncloud depths are forecast to increase to ~5,000 feet, but storm\nmotions will also be faster as mean wind/storm motions are\nforecast to increase to 20-25 mph. At the same time, instability\ngrows slightly on Thursday so storms may be more organized and \nintense (heavy rain but not much hail). We'll also have to watch \nthe timing of a shortwave expected for Thursday. The GFS remains \nslightly faster, while the EC is slightly slower and would be \nfavorably timed with peak daytime heating and convection. Whatever\nthe case, both days would at least have a couple ingredients \nfavorable for flash flooding, so we'll monitor that closely over \nthe next couple days before issuing any Flood Watches for flash \nflooding. \n\nLooking ahead to Friday and the weekend, there is more agreement\nnow that we'll be drying and stabilizing. This would focus mostly\non the plains with less storm coverage (mainly isolated near the \nhigher terrain), while mountain areas should be able to hold onto \nmore moisture and instability under the ridge. After a brief \nrespite from the heat and near normal temperatures Thursday, hot \nweather will likely return with highs pushing back to the 95-100 \ndegree range across the plains and I-25 Corridor, with potentially\nanother Heat Advisory by next Sunday.",
